# rules
Only Call Hooks at the Top Level, Don’t call Hooks inside loops, conditions, or nested functions. Don’t call Hooks from regular JavaScript functions. Instead, you can: Call Hooks from React function components; Call Hooks from custom Hooks

# useState
- defines state variable, value and setter function

const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
...
<button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>

# useEffect

- interacts with external systems
- replaces componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate, and componentWillUnmount, e.g.:
useEffect(() => {    
   function someHandlerFunction(status) {      
   ...}       
   // optional cleanup function
   return function cleanup() {      
   ...
   };  
});
- by default triggers with every rerender
- to trigger once
},[]);
- to trigger based on dependent variables
},[var1, var2]);

# useEffectEvent

- to extract non-reactive logic out of Effect
- e.g. to use the lates version of variable, not to react on its change
- limited, can be called only from useEffect

